                {"group": "Seams", "work": "rope", "seconds": 15, "optional": true, "raisesMetres": 0.15, "raisesVoxels": 2.4, "raisesLitres": 479.3, "raisesCubicVoxels": 1963.241}
            ],
            "sources": {
                "time": "A held action lasts the real time of that one piece of work over sixty: game time runs 30 times faster than real time, and boat work is halved again",
                "booms": "Setting one pole across her or up, about three minutes, as fitting one of a canoe's laths. A crooked pole, as Hawaiian and Marshallese booms curved down to the float (Young, 1876, marshall.csu.edu.au/Marshalls/html/culture/Canoes.html), takes its curve as it is laid: it rests on her gunwales and on the float's top, and settles with every pass as the float is hewn down beneath it",
                "hewing": "Six passes of the axe all round take the three logs down to the round float with pointed ends (bin/lib/hulls/outrigger.py). Chip by chip, an axe biting some 0.08 L a stroke 30 times a minute (H. Glaser, physiology of forestry work, FAO): some three hours of real work a pass. A pass takes as long as vanilla takes to fell a large oak with a flint axe, 30 s (ItemAxe: a log's break time times the root of the tree's size)",
                "lashing": "One rope is ten metres of lashing: about a quarter of an hour, as a canoe's (an estimate). Once the float is hewn, each boom is lashed to both gunwales and straight to the float, thirteen turns a lashing (ten round, three frapping) of their girth, counted by her shape's builder",
                "sail": "A crab claw between her two spars, as her model draws it (bin/lib/hulls/outrigger.py), one cloth a square metre, as one linen block covers: four, each laced to the spars in about a quarter of an hour (an estimate)",
                "stays": "A forestay, a backstay, a shroud out to the float and the sheet, each under ten metres: one rope each, set up in about a quarter of an hour (an estimate)",
                "strakes": "Optional, any time she is finished: one washstrake a side between her solid ends, a board some 15 cm high, as Pacific washstrakes ran 15 to 20 cm (a Tikopia hull 43 cm deep carries a 15 cm one, Haddon & Hornell, Canoes of Oceania, via collections.maa.cam.ac.uk/objects/555827; Hawaiian mo'o 6 to 8 in). Sawn planks, a metre a plank: 8 along her sides, and a board across each end of her hollow, where her solid ends begin, as the end pieces lashed on at Tobi and Kapingamarangi (Haddon & Hornell; Buck 1950), so the sea cannot come in over her low ends: 10. Fitting one, with its row of holes, about twenty minutes (an estimate)",
                "seams": "Sewn on through holes 3 in apart over a split batten (Haddon & Hornell, Mont-Dore canoe): each stitch about 30 cm of cord round wall, plank and batten, along her sides and her end boards, counted by her shape's builder (bin/lib/hulls/outrigger.py). Sewn, they raise her side by the strake's height and add the hold between them, by the model"
            },
